#section(Sommaire,Table of contents)

The Preferences program for ABackup works in the same way as the other Preferences
programs supplied with the Amiga Operating System. So, this documentation only
describes the differents parameters, and how they control ABackup processing.

It is suggested that you install this program in the "SYS:Prefs" directory,
together with the other Preferences programs. If you prefer to copy it otherwhere,
don't forget to update the "PREFSPATH" Tool Types in ABackup's icon, so it will be
able to find the Preference program.

#indent(4)
#menu(BackupOpt,RestoreOpt,VerifyOpt,CrunchOpt,TapeOpt,GUIOpt,ExternalOpt,MiscOpt)
#indent(0)

#section(BackupOpt,Backup options)

#ENUM(Backup to)
Specifies backup destination:

#indent(6)
#item
- a cyclic gadget let you select the archive type (device, file, tape)

#item
- two lists let you select the units to use, if the archive type is set to "Device".
The list on the left shows all the connected units, the one on the right shows the
units to use. To transfer a unit from one list to another, just click on it's name
in the list.

#item
- a string gadget, and a request calling button, let you select the name of the
archive file, if the archive type is set to "File".
#indent(0)

#ENUM(Buffer size)
Specifies the size of the buffer used by the child task for asynchronous writes,
as well as for streamer writes. When correctly tuned, this parameter can cause
an important increase in backup speed.

#ENUM(Log file)
If a filename is specified, a statistics line will be added in this file, each time
you make a backup. This line will show the backup date, time, source and destination,
the number of files backed up, the total number of bytes written, and the optional
comment associated to this backup.

#ENUM(Comment)
Specifies the default comment associated to the backup. See the "Add Comment" option
below.

#ENUM(Report)
Activates the creation of a backup report. This report may be send to the printer or
to a file. You can ask for a detailed or a brief report.

#ENUM(Verify)
Activates the verification of disk writes. This option slows down the backup, but
makes it safer. @{b}
#break
CAUTION ! Even when this option is activated, it is impossible to certify that the
backup is correct. If you want to make sure that the archive is 100% error free,
use the archive verify function of ABackup. @{ub}

#ENUM(Use child task)
Activates the use of a distinct task for disk writes, in order to perform asynchronous
writes. Depending on the size of the buffer (see above), this may cause an important
increase in backup speed.

#ENUM(Backup links)
Activates the backup of links created with the AmigaDOS "MakeLink" command.

#ENUM(Add Comment)
If this option is activated, ABackup will ask you to enter a small comment, at the
end of each backup. This comment will be displayed when opening the archive, before
a restore or a verify operation, and added in the backup log.

#ENUM(Add icon)
Activates the creation of an icon (".info" file) for archive files.

#ENUM(Compress data)
Activates data compression. ABackup won't try to compress files smaller than 512 bytes.
Files already compressed by Lha, Zoo, PowerPacker or XPK are detected, and not compressed.
See #xref(CrunchOpt).

#ENUM(Compress catalog)
Activates the compression of the archive catalog, with the same algorithm as the one used
for data compression.

#ENUM(Encrypt data)
Activates data encryption, with an user specified password you will be asked for before
backup start. @{b}
#break
CAUTION ! This password will be MANDATORY for any restore or verify operation. If you
forget it, backed up data will be definitively lost. Even the authors of the program
won't be able to help you to recover your data .
@{ub}

#ENUM(Set archive bit)
Activates the setting of the archive bit (see AmigaDOS "Protect" command) on every
successfully backed up file. This bit is reset by AmigaDOS, each time a file is modified,
so this will help you to perform incremental backups (modified files only), selecting
files by protection bits (see ABackup documentation).

#ENUM(Duplicate catalog)
If this option is activated, ABackup will duplicate the archive catalog (which is
normally added at the end of the archive) to a file. The name of this file may be
specified as an argument when starting ABackup. If not, it will be asked for at the
end of the backup.
#break
This option is very useful for tape backups, because you won't have to read all the
tape to fetch the catalog and then to rewind the tape to start the restore or verify
operation.

#ENUM(Ignore SKIPME)
This option allow to control if ABackup will look at directories comments. When
ABackup is scanning a directory tree, before backup selection, every directory
which comment begins by "SKIPME" isn't loaded. This feature avoids wasting lots
of time reading the contents of a large directory you never backup. There may be
times, however, when you want to backup even directories with this special comment.
#break
If this option is activated, every directory found will be loaded. If this option
isn't activated, directories which comment is beginning by "SKIPME" won't be loaded.

#justify(on)

#indent(0)
#section(RestoreOpt,Restore options)

#ENUM(Restore from)
Specifies the source archive, containing data to restore. For more information,
see the "Backup to" option in #xref(BackupOpt).

#ENUM(Restore to)
Specifies the directory where to restore files. If this gadget is empty, files
will be restored to the directory in which they where located at backup time.

#ENUM(Existing files)
Tells what to do when a file, which is about to be restored, already exists on your
hard disk. Possible actions are:

#indent(6)
#item
- Always replace (the existing file will be overwritten)
#item
- Never replace (the file won't be restored)
#item
- Ask before replacing (ABackup will ask you if you want to replace the file)
#item
- Replace if older (the existing file will be overwritten only if older than the
file in the archive)
#item
- Rename file (ABackup will ask you a new name for the file to restore)
#justify(on)

#ENUM(Bad files)
Tells what to do when ABackup failed to restore a file. In this case, the file on
your hard disk may be truncated, or damaged. Possible choices are:

#indent(6)
#item
- Always delete (the file will be deleted)
#item
- Never delete (the file will be kept)
#item
- Ask before deleting (ABackup will ask you if you want to delete the file)

#ENUM(Report)
Activates the creation of a restore report. This report may be send to the printer or
to a file. You can ask for a detailed or a brief report.

#ENUM(Directory tree)
Activates the restoration of the directory tree. If this option is deactivated, all
the files will be restored in the same directory.

#ENUM(Restore date)
When a file is restored, AmigaDOS gives it the restoration date, as it's last modification
date. If this option is activated, ABackup will set the date of the file to the date of
the file when it was backed up.

#ENUM(Restore link)
If this option is activated, ABackup will also restore links (see AmigaDOS "MakeLink"
command).

#ENUM(Empty directoris)
If this option is activated, ABackup will also restore empty directories.

#ENUM(Use catalog file)
If this option is activated, ABackup will load the archive catalog from a file. The name
of this file may be specified as an argument. If not, it will be asked for before loading
the archive catalog.

#indent(0)
#section(VerifyOpt,Verify options)

#ENUM(Report)
Activates the creation of a verify report. This report may be send to the printer or
to a file. You can ask for a detailed or a brief report.

#ENUM(Compare data)
By default, ABackup just tries to restore the files in the archive, but doesn't check
if data in the files is correct. However, as the program uses checksums, this is
quite a good verification. If this option is activated, ABackup will also compare
the restored data with the original data on your disk.
#break
This option slows down verification. Of course, ABackup will only be able to compare
files still located on your disk, and not modified. In order to detect an eventual
modification, the program will check both size and date of the files.

#ENUM(Ignore files date)
If this option is activated, ABackup won't check the date of the original file,
before comparison with the archived file.

#ENUM(Select files)
By default, ABackup verifies all the files in the archive. If this option is
activated, you will be able to select the file to verify, by the mean of the
usual selection window.

#indent(0)
#section(CrunchOpt,Compression options)

#ENUM(Method)
Specifies the compression algorithm to use. You can at least choose between "Internal"
(using ABackup internal compression) and "External" (using an external compression
program, see below). If the XPK library is intalled on your system, the "XPK library"
choice will be possible too.
#break
Please note that you can't use external compression for partition backups.

#ENUM(XPK Libs/XPK Mode)
If the selected compression method is "XPK library", these gadgets let you select
one of the XPK algorithm, and the mode for this algorithm.

#ENUM(Compressor/Decompressor)
These two gadgets let you specify the name and the arguments of compression and
decompression external programs. The naming conventions for arguments are:

#justify(off)
%0 (ou %s)    : full path name of the source file
%1            : path name of the source file
%2            : name of the source file (without path)
%3 (ou %d)    : full path name of the target file
%4            : path name of the target file
%5            : name of the target file (without path)
#justify(on)

As an example, here is the value to enter in these gadgets, if you want to use
the LhA program (copyright by Stefan Boberg):

#justify(off)
Compressor   : LhA a -X %d %s
Décompressor : LhA e -X %s %5 %4
#justify(on)

(You may omit the -X option for LhA, but then you will have to call ABackup with
the ECSUFFIX=.lha argument).

When using external compression, it is suggested to make these programs resident, or
to copy them in RAM:, in order to reduce the time required to load them in memory.
As these programs are called by ABackup for each file to compress or to decompress,
reducing loading time of one second means, for example, reducing backup time of 15
minutes, if you backup 1000 files.

When ABackup will run the external compression or decompression program, it will
send it's output to the "T:ABackup.log" file. You can look at this file to find
any problem that may occur during compression or decompression.

#ENUM(Filter)
Specifies a list of file's name extentions that will be excluded from compression.
For example, if the list contains "lzx", files which name ends by ".lzx" won't be
compressed.

#indent(0)
#section(TapeOpt,Tape options)

#ENUM(Device driver)
Specifies the name of the SCSI device driver (default: "scsi.device").

#ENUM(SCSI Port)
Specifies the SCSI port number of the tape drive.

#ENUM(Block size)
Specifies the block size supported by the tape drive.

#ENUM(Rewind)
Activates automatic tape rewind, at the end of the operation.

#ENUM(Auto retension)
Activates automatic tape retension, at the beginning of the operation.

#ENUM(Eject)
Activates automatic cardrige ejection, at the end of the operation. This function
is not supported by all tape drives.

#ENUM(FAST mem. buffer)
Allocates the buffer memory, used for input/output operations, in FAST memory instead
of CHIP memory.

#ENUM(Inquiry)
If the SCSI device driver and port number are correct, this gadget allows you to
get information from the tape driver. This information will be displayed in a
requester.

#indent(0)
#section(GUIOpt,Interface options)

#ENUM(Screen type)
Specifies the screen to use : the Workbench screen, a Custom screen, or a Public
screen.

#ENUM(Public screen name)
Specifies the name of the public screen to use, when the screen type is set to "Public"

#indent(0)
If the screen type is set to "Custom", you can select the mode for this screen. The
screen mode request used will be either the one of the asl.library, or the one of
the reqtools.library. If both libraries are missing (V38 or higher required),
you won't be able to change the screen mode.

#ENUM(Screen font)
Specifies screen font of the custom screen.

#ENUM(Text font)
Specifies text font for ABackup windows, whatever screen type is selected.

#ENUM(Palette)
If the screen type is set to "Custom", you can modify the palette of this
screen. The reqtools.library (V38 or higher) is required.

#indent(0)
#section(ExternalOpt,External programs options)

This window let you parameter files visualisation, which is started by a
double-click over a file's name, on the selection window of ABackup.

#ENUM(ASCII)
Name of the text files viewer program.

#ENUM(ILBM)
Name of the IFF pictures viewer program.

#ENUM(Other)
Name of the viewer program for the other file types.

#ENUM(Run asynchronously)
Tells to start the viewer program in the background, so ABackup won't
be locked until the viewing is finished.

#ENUM(Confirm each visualisation)
If this option is activated, ABackup will ask you confirmation before each
file viewing. This will allow you, if necessary, to modify the name of the
program to call.

#indent(0)
#section(MiscOpt,Miscellaneous options)

#ENUM(Alert)
Specifies how ABackup will warn you when an error occurs, or when it needs
some action from you. Possible choices are:

#indent(6)
#item
- None (ABackup will only display a requester)
#item
- Beep (ABackup will send a audible signal)
#item
- Flash screen (ABackup will make the screen flash)
#item
- Beep & flash (ABackup will send an audible signal AND flash the screen)

#ENUM(Files size)
Specifies the unit for files size: bytes, kilo-bytes, mega-bytes, or automatic
(select the highest possible unit).

#ENUM(Temporary directory)
Specifies the name of the working directory. For security reasons, it is not
recommanded to specify a directory located on a partition being backed up.

#ENUM(Default selection path)
Specifies the name of the directory in which the selection files are stored
(see ABackup documentation for more information about selection recording).

#ENUM(Print disk labels)
Activates automatic disk label printing, at the end of the backup.

#ENUM("Label length (lines)")
Specifies the number of lines per label.

#indent(0)
@endbase
